The Factory Method pattern almost always appears *inside* a Template Method pattern, and understanding this relationship is key to understanding why the pattern is designed the way it is.

The **Template Method pattern** defines a skeleton algorithm in a base class, with specific steps left as abstract methods for subclasses to implement. The base class calls `this.step1()`, `this.step2()`, `this.step3()` — a fixed sequence — but each `stepN()` is an overridable hook.

Look at `DocumentConverter.convert()` above:

```
convert(document) {
  const exporter = this.createExporter();  // ← factory method (hook)
  console.log(`Converting via ${exporter.mimeType}...`);
  const result = exporter.export(document);
  return { filename: document.title + exporter.extension, ...result };
}
```

`convert()` is a Template Method — it defines the fixed steps: get exporter, log, export, wrap result. `createExporter()` is the *hook* — the step that subclasses fill in. The factory method is the creation-specialised hook inside a template method.

This synergy explains a pattern you see in many frameworks: a base class provides rich functionality using `this.createXxx()` calls, and you subclass it just to override those factory methods. Spring Framework's `AbstractBeanFactory` is a 1,000-line template method that calls factory hooks throughout. NestJS's module system uses factory hooks for providers. React's class component `render()` method is literally a factory method for React elements.

**Why this matters in practice:** when you subclass a Creator to override its factory method, you get all the template logic 'for free' — the sequencing, the error handling, the logging the base class sets up. You only specify *what to create*, not *how to use it*. This is a powerful leverage point in framework design.

Classical Factory Method relies on class inheritance. JavaScript supports inheritance, but its dynamic nature makes it possible — and often more practical — to implement the same principle without a class hierarchy. The idiomatic JS approach is the **factory registry**.

Instead of a class hierarchy of creators, you maintain a map from type keys to factory functions:

```javascript
const exporterRegistry = {};

function registerExporter(type, factoryFn) {
  exporterRegistry[type] = factoryFn;
}

function createExporter(type) {
  const factory = exporterRegistry[type];
  if (!factory) throw new Error(`Unknown exporter type: "${type}"`);
  return factory();
}
```

Each call to `registerExporter` adds a new type without editing existing code. You can call it from anywhere — feature flags, plugin initialisation, test setup. Adding a new type is a single statement:

```javascript
registerExporter('epub', () => new EpubExporter());
```

This satisfies Open/Closed just as well as the class hierarchy approach. It is also more **composable**: you can pass a registry as a constructor argument, build different registries for different environments (a test registry that returns lightweight mock exporters), and inspect or iterate the available types at runtime.

The registry pattern is the backbone of most real-world extensible systems: plugin systems register their factories at startup; IoC containers store factory functions keyed by injection token; event systems map event names to handler factories.

**TypeScript enhancement:** in TypeScript you can type the registry with a generic to enforce that all registered factories return the correct Product type:

```typescript
type ExporterFactory = () => Exporter;
const exporterRegistry = new Map<string, ExporterFactory>();
```

This gives you full type-safety: the compiler will reject any factory that does not return an `Exporter`.

sections.code:
// ── Registry-based Factory Method (idiomatic JS/TS) ──────────────

// Product interface (using a class as an interface in JS)
class Logger {
  write(level, message) { throw new Error('write() not implemented'); }
  flush()              { /* optional */ }
}

// Concrete Products
class StdoutLogger extends Logger {
  write(level, message) {
    const prefix = `[${new Date().toISOString()}] [${level.toUpperCase()}]`;
    console.log(`${prefix} ${message}`);
  }
}

class FileLogger extends Logger {
  constructor(path) { super(); this._path = path; this._buffer = []; }
  write(level, message) {
    this._buffer.push(`${Date.now()} ${level} ${message}`);
  }
  flush() {
    // In real code: fs.appendFileSync(this._path, this._buffer.join('\n'));
    console.log(`[FileLogger] Flushing ${this._buffer.length} lines to ${this._path}`);
    this._buffer = [];
  }
}

class NullLogger extends Logger {
  write() { /* intentionally silent — useful for tests */ }
}

// ── Factory Registry ──────────────────────────────────────────────
const loggerRegistry = new Map();

function registerLogger(name, factoryFn) {
  if (loggerRegistry.has(name)) {
    throw new Error(`Logger "${name}" is already registered`);
  }
  loggerRegistry.set(name, factoryFn);
}

function createLogger(name, ...args) {
  const factory = loggerRegistry.get(name);
  if (!factory) {
    const available = [...loggerRegistry.keys()].join(', ');
    throw new Error(`Unknown logger: "${name}". Available: ${available}`);
  }
  return factory(...args);
}

// ── Register built-in types ───────────────────────────────────────
registerLogger('stdout', ()        => new StdoutLogger());
registerLogger('file',  (path)     => new FileLogger(path));
registerLogger('null',  ()         => new NullLogger());

// ── Creator class using the registry ─────────────────────────────
class AppLogger {
  constructor(loggerName, ...factoryArgs) {
    // Factory method delegated to the registry
    this._logger = createLogger(loggerName, ...factoryArgs);
  }
  info(msg)  { this._logger.write('info',  msg); }
  error(msg) { this._logger.write('error', msg); }
  warn(msg)  { this._logger.write('warn',  msg); }
  shutdown() { this._logger.flush(); }
}

// ── Client code ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
const devLogger  = new AppLogger('stdout');
const prodLogger = new AppLogger('file', '/var/log/production.log');
const testLogger = new AppLogger('null');    // silent in tests

devLogger.info('Application started');
prodLogger.error('Database timeout after 5000ms');
prodLogger.shutdown();
testLogger.error('This is silently discarded — perfect for unit tests');

// ── Adding a new logger type at runtime or plugin init ────────────
class RemoteLogger extends Logger {
  constructor(endpoint) { super(); this._endpoint = endpoint; }
  write(level, message) {
    // In real code: fetch(this._endpoint, { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({level, message}) });
    console.log(`[RemoteLogger → ${this._endpoint}] ${level}: ${message}`);
  }
}

// Registering the new type edits ZERO lines of existing code:
registerLogger('remote', (endpoint) => new RemoteLogger(endpoint));

const cloudLogger = new AppLogger('remote', 'https://logs.example.com/ingest');
cloudLogger.warn('Memory usage at 90%');

